    "culturalContext": "In Korean tradition, the sky (하늘, 天) has always been far more than physical space — it is a sacred realm inhabited by divine beings and the ultimate source of earthly authority. The founding mythology of Gojoseon describes Hwanung descending from heaven to govern the world, establishing from the very beginning that ascending into or flying through the sky symbolizes approaching divine power. Ancient sky-worship ceremonies (제천의식) — including Buyeo's Yonggo, Goguryeo's Dongmyeong, and Ye's Mucheon festivals — formally ritualized humanity's connection to heaven, reinforcing the sky as the source of all fortune and authority. In Korean shamanic tradition (무속), the shaman (무당) was understood as a being capable of flying to the heavens in an ecstatic trance state to communicate with spirits — making aerial flight the ultimate spiritual experience. The 태몽 (conception dream) tradition also records flying and heavenly descent as omens of extraordinary births. The most famous account in the Samguksagi describes the mother of the Silla general Gim Yusin dreaming of a golden-armored youth riding clouds down from heaven — she subsequently conceived one of Korea's greatest military heroes. This centuries-deep cultural framework is why Koreans today still treat a flying dream as something worth paying attention to the morning after.",
    "westernContext": "Western psychology has arrived at remarkably parallel — and interestingly different — conclusions about flying dreams. Freud, in 'The Interpretation of Dreams' (1900), interpreted aerial flight as symbolic expression of childhood physical sensations (changing breath rhythms, muscle relaxation during sleep) combined with repressed drives. For Freud, the soaring ego represents wish fulfillment: the desire to escape the constraints of daily life and break free from the controlling superego. Carl Jung took a broader view, connecting flying dreams to the 'ascent' archetype embedded in the collective unconscious — a universal motif found across world mythology and religion. For Jung, flight symbolizes the soul's transcendence of material limitations and the individuation process: the ego's aspiration to integrate with the deeper Self. Modern neuroscience offers a more mechanical explanation: flying dreams arise primarily during REM sleep when the vestibular system and proprioception signals are reinterpreted by the dreaming brain. Lucid dream researcher Stephen LaBerge demonstrated that flying is one of the most common experiences in lucid dreaming states, and studies indicate that people with high self-efficacy — strong belief in their own capabilities — report flying dreams more frequently than others. The fundamental contrast between Korean and Western interpretation lies in directionality. Korean tradition treats flying dreams as prophetic signals about external events to come; Western psychology reads them as the dreamer's inner language — expressing repressed desires, the need for freedom, or the state of self-belief. What both agree on, across every framework: flying in a dream is connected to transcendence, the urge to exceed limits, and the deep human longing to rise above what currently constrains us.",
