    "culturalContext": "In Korean folk dream interpretation, the car-driving dream is a thoroughly modern symbol that inherits the cultural weight once carried by horse-riding and carriage dreams in classical lore. Just as riding a horse symbolized vitality and one's life trajectory, driving a car represents agency over one's social role, livelihood, and forward momentum. In Korean folk tradition, the vehicle often stands directly in for the dreamer's current workplace, business, or life situation — the condition of the car and the ease of driving mirror the state of that endeavor with remarkable precision. Cars also carry strong connotations of social status and financial success in Korean culture, so dreaming of driving a fine car with skill is frequently read as a sign of prospering wealth and rising fortune.",
    "westernContext": "Western psychology has long recognized the driving dream as one of the richest available metaphors for selfhood and control. From a Freudian perspective, the car is an extension of the ego — a vehicle (in every sense) for libidinal energy and impulse. The act of driving represents the ongoing negotiation between desire and restraint: brake failure may signal suppressed impulses breaking free from ego control, while high-speed driving can represent the discharge of pent-up energy or frustration. Freud might also note the car's association with masculine power and social authority.\n\nJungian psychology reframes this elegantly. The car represents ego-consciousness itself — the aspect of the psyche we most consciously inhabit. How the car behaves mirrors the dreamer's self-image and psychological energy. Steering your own car is an expression of active individuation: you are authoring your own journey. Being driven by someone else suggests that an external influence, a shadow element, or an internal complex is currently calling the shots. An open highway, in this reading, is a phase in which the individuation process is flowing cleanly, with purpose and momentum.\n\nModern cognitive psychology takes a more pragmatic view. Research classifying thousands of dream records finds that roughly 40% of car-related dream events involve loss of control — and these dreams spike in frequency during periods of genuine high stress, overwhelming responsibility, or emotional overload. Crucially, they occur even in people who have never driven, confirming that the car functions as a universal metaphor for life management, not a literal symbol drawn from driving experience.\n\nCross-culturally, the driving dream reveals a meaningful difference in worldview. Western cultures tend to read the car as a symbol of individual freedom and personal autonomy — the open road as pure liberation. Korean and East Asian traditions more strongly link the vehicle to social role and community standing. And where Western psychology reads the dream inward as a reflection of unconscious states, Korean folk interpretation reads it outward as a potential omen of future events. The same image, two different questions: what does this say about where I am now, versus what is this telling me about what comes next?",
