    "culturalContext": "Fishing has been woven into Korean life since the Neolithic period — stone fishing hooks discovered in Yangyang, Gangwon Province, date back approximately 4,500 years. Historical records note that Silla's fourth king fished for sustenance before ascending to the throne, illustrating fishing's deep roots across all social strata.\n\nDuring the Joseon Dynasty, Confucian scholars including Yi Hwang, Yi I, and Yun Sondo elevated fishing to a meditative discipline, composing poetry and paintings that celebrated the act as a bridge between the scholar-official and the natural world. In Korean folk tradition, fish embody prosperity and social advancement: celadon and buncheong pottery featuring paired fish carried aspirations for passing both stages of the civil service examination — the highest form of career success in premodern Korea.\n\nIn Korean Buddhism, fish — because they never close their eyes — symbolize perpetual wakefulness and spiritual vigilance, which is why a wooden fish (목어) hangs in every temple. The auspicious quality of fishing dreams draws on this millennia-long symbolic heritage: the patient cast and eventual catch mirrors the Confucian ideal of disciplined effort yielding righteous reward, a philosophy that remains deeply resonant in Korean cultural values today.",
    "westernContext": "From a Western psychological perspective, the fishing dream carries a richly layered symbolic vocabulary that converges, in fascinating ways, with the Korean interpretive tradition.\n\nFreudian analysis frames water as the reservoir of emotion and unconscious drive — the libidinal depths beneath the surface of conscious awareness. The act of casting a line and drawing something up from below becomes a metaphor for surfacing repressed desires or suppressed impulses. A successful catch may represent the fulfillment of something long denied; failed fishing can reflect frustration in intimate relationships or blocked desire seeking expression.\n\nJungian psychology offers a deeper framework. For Jung, fish swimming in the depths of the water are denizens of the collective unconscious — symbols of wisdom, instinct, and undiscovered aspects of the Self. Fishing, then, is the individuation process made literal: the deliberate, patient effort to bring unconscious contents into the light of awareness. When you catch a fish in a dream, Jung would suggest you are successfully integrating a previously hidden dimension of your own psyche. The waiting inherent in fishing mirrors the receptive, unhurried quality that genuine psychological growth demands — you cannot force the fish to bite.\n\nContemporary psychology reads fishing dreams through the lens of goal-directed behavior and delayed gratification. A successful catch correlates strongly with high self-efficacy — confidence in your capacity to achieve meaningful goals through sustained effort. Recurring dreams of failed fishing, by contrast, may surface anxiety or a felt sense of powerlessness around a high-stakes ongoing pursuit, a signal worth sitting with rather than ignoring.\n\nCross-culturally, the resonance is striking. In Chinese tradition, the word for fish (鱼, yú) is a homophone for abundance (余), making fish dreams inherently auspicious; fishing specifically represents resisting temptation and earning legitimate rewards. Japanese culture venerates the koi for ascending waterfalls against all odds — a symbol of perseverance transforming into transcendence. In Christian tradition, Jesus calling his disciples 'fishers of men' lends fishing the weight of spiritual vocation and conscious awakening. Across every tradition, the message converges: fish patiently, prepare well, and trust that what you seek is rising toward you.",
