    "culturalContext": "Korean culture holds a unique and ancient relationship with birth dreams through the tradition of taemong (태몽) — prophetic dreams believed to herald conception and reveal the character, gender, and destiny of an unborn child. This tradition is documented as far back as the Samguk Yusa (Memoranda of the Three Kingdoms, 13th century CE), one of Korea's foundational historical texts. The birth of General Kim Yu-sin, one of Silla's greatest military figures, is famously accompanied by his mother's taemong account.\n\nTraditional folk interpretation held that dreaming of celestial beings — dragons, the sun, stars, or phoenixes — signaled the coming birth of a son with great destiny, while dreams of flowers, shells, or delicate natural objects foretold a daughter. These weren't idle superstitions; during the Goryeo and Joseon dynasties, taemong accounts associated with royal births were recorded in official court documents, reflecting the Confucian philosophy of 천인감응 (cheon-in-gamung, 'Heaven-Human resonance') — the belief that significant births were cosmically meaningful events foreshadowed by Heaven itself.\n\nToday, while scientific rationalism has softened formal belief in taemong, the cultural practice of sharing and interpreting birth dreams remains vibrantly alive in Korean society. Pregnancy communities online buzz with taemong accounts, and grandmothers still eagerly interpret the dreams of expectant mothers. The tradition persists not because Koreans uniformly believe in literal prophecy, but because it provides a meaningful ritual framework for the momentous experience of bringing new life into the world.",
    "westernContext": "Western psychological traditions offer a rich counterpoint to Korean folk interpretation of birth dreams, illuminating different but complementary dimensions of meaning.\n\nFrom a Freudian perspective, birth dreams symbolize the eruption of repressed creative urges or maternal instincts into conscious awareness. They may also reflect unconscious desires or anxieties around pregnancy and parenthood, as well as the psychological complexity of separating a part of oneself into an independent being. Freud saw birth and separation as foundational psychic experiences — dreaming of birth can resurface unresolved feelings about one's own entry into the world, as well as ambivalence about creation and responsibility.\n\nJungian psychology treats birth dreams as central symbols of the individuation process — the lifelong journey toward psychological wholeness. The newborn represents a new aspect of the self emerging from the unconscious: latent potential breaking through, a dormant capacity awakening, or a psychic leap toward greater integration. Jung believed that anyone, regardless of gender, could experience this psychic 'birth' through the creative principle he called the anima or animus. From this angle, dreaming of giving birth is one of the most potent signals that deep transformation is underway.\n\nModern cognitive and clinical psychology interprets birth dreams more practically: they often function as mental rehearsal for major transitions — launching a creative project, stepping into a new role, or navigating a significant life change. The quality of the delivery in the dream (smooth vs. difficult, healthy baby vs. complication) tends to mirror the dreamer's confidence level and underlying anxieties about embracing vulnerability and change.\n\nCross-culturally, birth dreams occupy a universal place in the human symbolic vocabulary — virtually every tradition associates birth with new beginnings. The key difference lies in emphasis: Western frameworks spotlight individual psychological transformation and creative expression, while Korean and broader East Asian traditions read birth dreams through a collective lens of family prosperity, social achievement, and communal fortune. Neither lens is wrong; they simply illuminate different facets of the same profound human experience.",
